<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>

  <!--
    Inject the per-bench "Gather ingredients" switch (issue #230) onto every bill-giving building.

    WHY THE ANCHOR IS inspectorTabs/li="ITab_Bills" AND NOT thingClass="Building_WorkTable":
    an xpath thingClass predicate is a LITERAL STRING match, so it only ever hits defs spelled exactly
    "Building_WorkTable" — it silently misses every SUBCLASS. In vanilla Core alone that would drop
    Building_WorkTable_HeatPush, which is the electric stove, the fuelled stove, the stonecutter's table,
    the electric smelter and the electric crematorium — i.e. exactly the stove the feature was requested
    for — plus every modded bench that subclasses either one. "Has a bills tab" is the honest test for
    "this building takes crafting bills", and it is what Common Sense anchors its own per-bench toggle on.

    THE COST is deliberate over-reach: a few bill givers that HD never routes anyway (mech gestators and
    other autonomous worktables) also receive the comp. That is harmless — the comp is an inert scribed
    bool, and CompBenchGather.CompGetGizmosExtra hides the button on anything BillRouteGate.IsRoutableBenchType
    rejects, so no button ever appears where the gather did not apply in the first place.

    OPERATION ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, for two reasons that are worth stating precisely:
      * PatchOperationSequence.ApplyWorker returns false at the FIRST failing child and runs no more of them.
        So the operation that is most likely to match nothing must come LAST, or it would silently cancel the
        ones after it.
      * <success>Always</success> on the top-level sequence is what stops THAT abort from being reported as a
        failed patch — an xpath matching nothing is a normal outcome here, not an error.
    The three operations:
      1. PatchOperationTest — present for parity with Common Sense's own per-bench toggle patch, which this
         file mirrors. Be honest about what it does NOT buy: a failing child Add does not log anything.
         PatchOperation.Apply only logs through Complete(modIdentifier), LoadedModManager calls Complete over
         the flat list of TOP-LEVEL operations in ModContentPack.Patches, and PatchOperationSequence.Complete
         does not recurse into children — so a child Add that matches nothing simply returns false, quietly.
         The Test is therefore inert in the case it appears to guard (if op 2 would fail, op 3 is skipped
         either way), and it can only ever subtract: a Test failure would also skip the op 3 fallback below.
      2. Add into the existing <comps> — covers every bill giver that already has comps (nearly all of them).
      3. Add a WHOLE <comps> node to the bill givers that have none. LAST, per the first rule above: in a
         typical modlist this matches nothing, and aborting at the final operation skips nothing.
    (HD's own HaulersDream_Pawns.xml has no such fallback and simply skips comps-less defs; this mirrors
    Common Sense's more complete shape instead, so a bench def without comps still gets the switch.)
  -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <success>Always</success>
    <operations>

      <li Class="PatchOperationTest">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[inspectorTabs/li="ITab_Bills"]/comps</xpath>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[inspectorTabs/li="ITab_Bills"]/comps</xpath>
        <value>
          <li><compClass>HaulersDream.CompBenchGather</compClass></li>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[inspectorTabs/li="ITab_Bills" and not(comps)]</xpath>
        <value>
          <comps>
            <li><compClass>HaulersDream.CompBenchGather</compClass></li>
          </comps>
        </value>
      </li>

    </operations>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
